{
190: {skill_tested: "Identify an omitted detail that affected the narrator's result.", question: "Which information absent from the recipe card first explains the narrator's unsuccessful cake?", option1: "The name of the community cookbook", option2: "The color of the original handwriting", option3: "The number of digital archive tags", option4: "The use of a shallow pan", correctAnswer: "The use of a shallow pan", explanation: "Step 1: The narrator follows the written ingredients and commands but the cake cooks unevenly.<br><br>Step 2: The aunt immediately asks whether the narrator used the shallow pan, which the card never names.<br><br>Answer: The use of a shallow pan", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
191: {skill_tested: "Determine the meaning of a word in context.", question: "As used in the sixth paragraph, 'facsimile' most nearly means:", option1: "a close reproduction of the original card.", option2: "a recipe rewritten with standardized units.", option3: "a recording of a cook preparing a dish.", option4: "an index arranged by cooking technique.", correctAnswer: "a close reproduction of the original card.", explanation: "Step 1: The facsimile approach preserves handwriting, stains, and crossed-out ingredients.<br><br>Step 2: Those features belong to the original artifact and are retained through a close reproduction.<br><br>Answer: a close reproduction of the original card.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
192: {skill_tested: "Identify the central idea of an informational passage.", question: "Which statement best expresses the passage's central idea?", option1: "Historical recipes should be replaced by standardized modern instructions.", option2: "A written recipe preserves part of a practice, but understanding and carrying it forward also require context, judgment, and acknowledged variation.", option3: "A recipe succeeds only when every cook produces an identical dish.", option4: "Digital recordings can preserve cooking practices without selecting or omitting information.", correctAnswer: "A written recipe preserves part of a practice, but understanding and carrying it forward also require context, judgment, and acknowledged variation.", explanation: "Step 1: The card omits tools, conditions, and embodied judgments.<br><br>Step 2: Editorial and digital methods preserve different aspects but none saves everything.<br><br>Answer: A written recipe preserves part of a practice, but understanding and carrying it forward also require context, judgment, and acknowledged variation.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
193: {skill_tested: "Analyze the function of a governing metaphor.", question: "The comparison of a recipe card to a musical score primarily emphasizes that a recipe:", option1: "must be performed with instruments rather than kitchen tools.", option2: "is intended to produce a different result every time.", option3: "guides action while leaving some decisions to knowledge and interpretation.", option4: "contains every motion needed for a successful performance.", correctAnswer: "guides action while leaving some decisions to knowledge and interpretation.", explanation: "Step 1: A score guides a performance without containing every motion or judgment.<br><br>Step 2: The passage says cooks following the same words may make different dishes because performers supply knowledge and choices.<br><br>Step 3: The metaphor shifts the recipe from a complete container to a partial guide for action.<br><br>Answer: guides action while leaving some decisions to knowledge and interpretation.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
194: {skill_tested: "Explain the author's qualification of a metaphor.", question: "Why does the author state that the musical-score comparison has limits?", option1: "Musical scores always contain less detail than recipe cards.", option2: "Abbreviated recipes deliberately invite every possible interpretation.", option3: "Family recipes cannot be written on paper.", option4: "Recipe omissions may reflect an assumed audience and can exclude cooks who lack access to shared instruction.", correctAnswer: "Recipe omissions may reflect an assumed audience and can exclude cooks who lack access to shared instruction.", explanation: "Step 1: A score may intentionally permit interpretation.<br><br>Step 2: A recipe writer may omit knowledge only because an unfamiliar reader was never anticipated.<br><br>Step 3: The missing context can become an access barrier rather than a creative invitation.<br><br>Answer: Recipe omissions may reflect an assumed audience and can exclude cooks who lack access to shared instruction.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Author's Purpose and Perspective", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
195: {skill_tested: "Compare the tradeoffs among preservation approaches.", question: "The three community-cookbook approaches are presented mainly to show that:", option1: "each method preserves certain kinds of information while sacrificing or altering others.", option2: "standardized recipes preserve handwriting better than facsimiles do.", option3: "commentary eliminates all uncertainty about a dish.", option4: "editors should choose one universal method for every collection.", correctAnswer: "each method preserves certain kinds of information while sacrificing or altering others.", explanation: "Step 1: Standardization helps repeatability, facsimile preserves the object, and commentary captures context and variation.<br><br>Step 2: The author explicitly concludes that no approach saves everything.<br><br>Step 3: The list makes preservation a choice among values rather than a complete transfer.<br><br>Answer: each method preserves certain kinds of information while sacrificing or altering others.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
196: {skill_tested: "Infer why the narrator's added notes remain provisional.", question: "The narrator's new notes are not final primarily because:", option1: "the narrator refuses to record any measurements.", option2: "differences in ovens and ingredients will continue to require adaptation.", option3: "the aunt no longer remembers which pan to use.", option4: "the original card prohibits later annotations.", correctAnswer: "differences in ovens and ingredients will continue to require adaptation.", explanation: "Step 1: The narrator adds more explicit observations after the second attempt.<br><br>Step 2: The narrator's oven and available oranges differ from the aunt's.<br><br>Step 3: New detail improves guidance but cannot eliminate changing conditions for future cooks.<br><br>Answer: differences in ovens and ingredients will continue to require adaptation.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
197: {skill_tested: "Evaluate the passage's treatment of completeness.", question: "How does the warning about digital abundance extend the passage's larger argument?", option1: "It shows that adding more media can preserve context without ever involving selection.", option2: "It argues that searchable tags are less useful than stains on paper in every case.", option3: "It shows that a richer archive may still omit choices and disagreements while appearing comprehensive.", option4: "It proves that audio recordings are identical to handwritten commands.", correctAnswer: "It shows that a richer archive may still omit choices and disagreements while appearing comprehensive.", explanation: "Step 1: Audio, versions, and tags can add context beyond a card.<br><br>Step 2: Recordings still select what to show, and tags may conceal disagreement.<br><br>Step 3: More material can therefore deepen the record without making it complete.<br><br>Answer: It shows that a richer archive may still omit choices and disagreements while appearing comprehensive.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
198: {skill_tested: "Synthesize the passage's final conception of preservation.", question: "The description of the recipe as an invitation to reconstruct a practice suggests that preservation succeeds best when it:", option1: "freezes one version and prevents future cooks from recording changes.", option2: "removes all instructions that rely on a cook's judgment.", option3: "guarantees that ingredients remain identical across locations and seasons.", option4: "retains a usable connection among the artifact, its context, and later informed adaptations.", correctAnswer: "retains a usable connection among the artifact, its context, and later informed adaptations.", explanation: "Step 1: The card points toward knowledge distributed among tools, ingredients, habits, and people.<br><br>Step 2: Preserving only paper or one authoritative version would lose parts of that practice.<br><br>Step 3: Context plus documented variation allows later cooks to participate responsibly in its continuation.<br><br>Answer: retains a usable connection among the artifact, its context, and later informed adaptations.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"}
}
